<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  HARD SHIPPING GATE — see 02-native-sdk-overview.md §5.1.

  Since 1 May 2024, an SDK using a required-reason API must declare it in a privacy
  manifest or App Store Connect rejects the upload with ITMS-91053 (Missing API
  declaration). That rejection lands on the MERCHANT's upload, not ours: shipping this
  file wrong or absent breaks every integrator's release, which is the worst possible
  first impression.

  Known failure mode to validate before release (03-implementation-strategy.md §3.1):
  AppsFlyer's manifest failed to bundle correctly in the *static* SPM variant (their
  issue #281). Propagation must be checked against a real consumer app, not just a
  local `swift build`.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      No tracking as defined by App Tracking Transparency: nothing here is joined with
      data from other companies' apps or sites, and no data goes to a data broker. The
      ATT question itself is 02 §12 open question 1 and needs a legal decision before
      iOS ships; if that decision changes, this key, NSPrivacyTrackingDomains, and the
      NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking flags below all change with it.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array>
        <!--
          The anonymous id. Apple's "Device ID" covers any identifier scoped to a
          particular device, browser or app installation, which is exactly what this
          is: derived from a P-256 keypair the platform holds, one per installation,
          gone on uninstall (02 §4).

          Linked, because the whole point of the install handoff is that the wallet
          links this id to the user's Frak identity. Declaring it unlinked would be
          false the moment `openFrakApp()` succeeds.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
        <!--
          `trackPurchase(customerId:orderId:token:)` transmits the merchant's own
          customer and order identifiers plus a checkout token, so a payout can be
          reconciled. That is purchase history under Apple's taxonomy even though no
          amount or line item is sent. Over-declaring is not a rejection;
          under-declaring is.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurchaseHistory</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
    <!--
      UserDefaults is one of the five required-reason categories, and this SDK stores
      both the resolved merchant config and the anonymous id there (02 §4, and the
      storage row of the §5 dependency table). Nothing else the SDK touches is a
      required-reason API: the event queue is an ordinary file whose timestamps are
      never read, and neither WebKit nor UIPasteboard is in a category.

      CA92.1 = access scoped to the app itself. Verify against Apple's live reason
      list before release — 02 §5.1 notes C56D.1 (SDK reads/writes only its own
      namespace) may be the better fit and is what many third-party SDKs ship.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>CA92.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
